  ================================================================
  WHY THIS MODULE IS ARCHIVED
  ================================================================
  The migration must preserve security and permission-evaluation outcomes. The permission
  evaluator and the reflection-based scanning that supports it are unchanged by directive, and
  this test class exercises the access-decision path directly and at greater breadth than any
  other report in this archive, which makes it the single most behaviour-bearing unit test
  report in the whole of it. It loads and evaluates access rules held as a configured rule
  list and matched against a stored search document: a missing rule list and an empty rule
  list, a permission that does not match and a target type that does not match, a status
  expressed as an array inside the rule, an inherited parent fallback action, requirements that
  demand every listed authority and requirements that demand any one of them, each of those in
  a wildcard suffixed form and in a case-insensitive form, and participant-type evaluation for
  an assignee principal, for a reader principal, for a principal holding one of several
  required group types, for a principal holding none of the required types, and for the two
  degenerate participant-type lists.
  CONSEQUENCE: a difference between this baseline report and its migrated counterpart is not a
  minor regression that can be absorbed. It is a security outcome change, and it is blocking.
  The comparison bar for this class is effectively absolute on outcomes, because an
  authorisation test that changes state is by definition a security outcome change: there is no
  reading of an altered access decision that is cosmetic, and no aggregate pass rate that
  redeems one.
